{
  "paraId": "480C9F2B",
  "style": "",
  "text": "In this trial, radiotherapy was administered during ICI maintenance after chemoimmunotherapy. Platinum-based chemoimmunotherapy achieves maximal tumor response in the induction phase but carries substantial toxicity. Thus, most novel ICI combinations are increasingly investigated in the maintenance setting 38–40. Our sequential design was rationally constructed to avoid additive toxicity from concurrent chemo-immunoradiotherapy and minimize target volumes by delivering focal radiotherapy in the setting of reduced tumor burden. This optimized treatment sequence appears to account for both the promising survival efficacy and the favorable safety outcomes. By contrast, in the phase 3 KEYLYNK-006 trial, Gray et al. reported that substituting olaparib for pemetrexed as the maintenance partner of pembrolizumab after chemoimmunotherapy did not improve outcomes: among the 672 of 1003 induction-treated patients who could be randomized, median progression-free survival was 7.1 months (95% CI, 5.6–8.7) with pembrolizumab plus olaparib versus 8.3 months (95% CI, 6.9–11.5) with pembrolizumab plus pemetrexed at a median follow-up of 39.9 months, suggesting that changing the maintenance systemic backbone alone confers little benefit, whereas adding tumor-directed radiotherapy in our sequential design was accompanied by the favorable efficacy and safety described above. (2025)38"
}

